{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Say, and you shall say</b> — to admonish the adults concerning the minors. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 21:1), from the words of our Sages (Yevamot 114a). And this admonition is to say that we must not assist the minors in becoming defiled with our hands, and through this many admonitions in the Torah are derived, according to the midrash of our Sages (ibid.) — in blood, in creeping things, and in impurity — and from them it is inferred for all prohibitions in the Torah, that we must not assist in any of them so that the minors transgress them. But if they act on their own initiative, we are not commanded to separate them from it.\n\nAnd the reason for the verse according to its midrash is: \"Say to the priests\" — and turn back and say to them that they shall not defile themselves. And the multiplication of admonitions, that He should command to admonish them, is so that all the sons of Aaron will be careful in this matter, even the minors.\n\nBut Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 21:1) said, according to the plain sense: \"Say to the priests\" — the section mentioned above, for they are the teachers of Torah and those who admonish the people; \"and you shall say to them\" — the commandments that they alone are obligated to observe. And this is not correct.\n\nIn my opinion, according to the plain sense of the verse, the meaning of \"say\" (אֱמֹר) is like \"speak\" (דַּבֵּר): \"Give ear to my words (אֲמָרַי), O YHWH\" (Psalms 5:2) — my speech; and likewise \"words (אֲמָרִים) of truth\" (Proverbs 22:21); and likewise \"for she heard all the words (אִמְרֵי) of YHWH\" (Joshua 24:27). And it is written, \"Come to Pharaoh and speak (וְדִבַּרְתָּ) to him\" (Exodus 9:1), which is like \"and you shall say\" (וְאָמַרְתָּ); and \"Moses spoke (וַיְדַבֵּר) to Aaron and to Eleazar and to Ithamar his sons who remained, saying: Take the meal offering\" (Leviticus 10:12), which is like \"and he said\" (וַיֹּאמֶר). And similarly in many places: \"Speak to the children of Israel and say to them\" — this is said in sections where He wishes to admonish them greatly, whether because of the severity of the matter or because they are accustomed to sin in it. And so He says, \"Say to the priests and say to them\"; and like it, \"Thus says YHWH, the Elohim of Israel: Go and say to Zedekiah king of Judah and say to him: Thus says YHWH…\" (Jeremiah 34:2), whose meaning is: speak to Zedekiah. And likewise, \"And the woman of Tekoa said to the king, and she fell on her face to the ground and bowed and said, 'Save, O king'\" (II Samuel 14:4), which is like: and the woman spoke to the king and said, 'Save, O king.'\n\nBehold, the meaning of \"Say to the priests and say to them\" is like the meaning of \"Speak to the priests and say to them\"; \"Speak to the children of Israel and say to them.\" Its meaning is: speak to the people in My name and say to them thus, like the matter of \"Come to Pharaoh and say to him\" (Exodus 7:26). And many will say that the meaning of \"Speak to the children of Israel\" is like the meaning of a call — speak to the children of Israel that they should gather to you and say to them thus; and likewise \"Say to the priests\" — that they should assemble and hear.\n\nAnd the reason for \"the priests\" is that in commandments that concern the offerings, it says \"to Aaron and to his sons\" and does not call them by the name \"the priests,\" because those matters concern the offerings or the ascent of the sanctuary. But here He admonishes that they shall not defile themselves through the dead ever, even at a time when they do not enter the sanctuary, and this is a distinction for them in themselves. Therefore the verse mentions \"the priests,\" to say that because they are the priests of YHWH and the ministers of our Elohim, He says to them that they should conduct themselves with honor and greatness in themselves and not defile themselves ever. And behold, the profaned ones (חֲלָלִים, i.e., disqualified priests of blemished lineage) are excluded from this general category.\n\nAnd the meaning of \"he shall not defile himself\" (לֹא יִטַּמָּא) is that you shall say to them that no one of them shall defile himself for a soul among his people; or it is drawn to \"a husband (בַּעַל) among his people\" mentioned below, and its versification is: לְנֶפֶשׁ לֹא יִטַּמָּא הַבַּעַל בְּעַמָּיו (\"for a soul he shall not defile himself, the husband among his people\").\n\nAnd the meaning of \"a husband (בַּעַל) among his people\" is like \"from the lords (בַּעֲלֵי) of Judah\" (I Samuel 6:2), \"the lords (בַּעֲלֵי) of the nations\" (Isaiah 16:8) — those who are esteemed among them or the masters — from the language of \"its owner (בְּעָלָיו) is not with it\" (Exodus 22:13), \"the master (בַּעַל) of the house\" (Exodus 22:7), for the esteemed are called masters. He says: an esteemed man among his people shall not defile himself, to profane his honor. The verse explains that because of the eminence of the priest — since he is worthy of being the great and esteemed one among his people — He admonishes him not to profane his eminence through corpse-impurity.\n\nAnd the verse thereby intends that it should not arise in our minds to say that the admonition applies only when they come to the Tent of Meeting to minister in the sanctuary. And similarly throughout the entire section He admonishes that everything pertains to their eminence: \"She profanes her father\" (Leviticus 21:9), \"and he shall not profane his seed\" (Leviticus 21:15). And likewise the translation of Onkelos: לָא יִסְתָּאַב רַבָּא בְּעַמֵּהּ לְאַחָלוּתֵיהּ (\"a great man among his people shall not defile himself, to profane himself\"). And in Sifrei Kehunata (the Sifra) it is expounded as referring to one who is a husband (בַּעַל) with his wife, as Rashi wrote."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ובמ\"כ is resolved as referring to the Sifra (ספרא), known in Rabbinic literature by this acronym; the citation \"אמור פרשה א טו\" follows the standard Sifra chapter/paragraph numbering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:32.906550619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}